                    (1, "heading", "ffor the Dropsie", "For the dropsy", ""),
                    (2, "body", "℞ sem[en] ʒß anissede, licorice, & fenell seede, mace, Safron ana ʒij caroweye seede, fennell seede, & spikenard ana ʒj", "Take 1/2 dram aniseed, licorice, fennel seed, mace, saffron 2 drams each, caraway seed, fennel seed, and spikenard 1 dram each;", ""),
                    (3, "body", "make them into fine pulder & eate off that pulder in porege: or drink of it w[i]t[h] posset ale a spoonfull at once.", "make them into fine powder and eat of that powder in pottage, or drink of it with posset ale a spoonful at once.", ""),
                    (4, "heading", "ffor the Dropsie", "For the dropsy", ""),
                    (5, "body", "℞ Sage & cresses ana a good quantitie: make a coffin of wheaten dough & fill git w[i]t[h] the herbes & cet bake it well: then break git into three gallonds of Ale all hott and cover the drinke close and keepe the drinke therof at y[e] xld afore[said]. Mr Colt.", "Take sage and cresses of each a good quantity; make a coffin [crust] of wheaten dough and fill it with the herbs and bake it well; then break it into 3 gallons of ale all hot and cover it close and drink thereof as aforesaid. Mr. Colt.", ""),
                    (6, "heading", "Mirabile est Lixiuium factu[m] ex cinerib[us] vitis potatu[m] mane, purgat humores aquosos in Hidrope p[ri]us tamen dequoquatur in aq[ua] eupatorij radicib[us] petroselini q[ue] sunt appropriata Hepati.", "Wonderful is a lye made from vine-ashes drunk in the morning: it purges watery humours in dropsy, provided it is first boiled in water of agrimony and parsley roots which are proper to the liver.", "Latin."),
                    (7, "body", "Balneum aque Aluminose refert H.D.", "A bath of alum-water [helps], reports H.D. [Henry Dineley].", "Latin note signed H.D."),
                    (8, "heading", "vnguentu[m] ad ventrem", "Ointment for the belly", ""),
                    (9, "body", "℞ Ol. absinthij, de spica ana ʒij ... Bdellij ʒj cer[e] q.s. f[iat] vng[uentum] ad ventrem.", "Take oil of wormwood, of spikenard 2 oz each... bdellium 1 dram, wax q.s., make an ointment for the belly.", ""),
                    (10, "heading", "Tympanites Hidropis", "Tympanites dropsy", ""),
                    (11, "body", "℞ furfures & ita callidas extende sup[er] pannu[m] tegendo alio panno in forma sacculi: & appone factu[m] cu[m] puluere cimini & callidu[m] appone toto ventri tub[...].", "Take bran and spread it hot upon a cloth, covering with another cloth in the form of a little bag, made with cumin powder, and apply hot to the whole belly.", ""),
                    (12, "body", "In tympanite appone ton[... ]audia p[er] vng[uen]ta molla &c.", "In tympanites apply... soft ointments, etc.", ""),
                    (13, "heading", "Contra Inflat[iones] pedu[m] & crur[ium] in Hidrope", "Against swellings of the feet and legs in dropsy", ""),
                    (14, "body", "℞ Conizas cu[m] com[un]ib[us] sibi doi flor[ibus] & co[n]tunde optime & longa agitacione ad spissitud[inem] empl[astri] & appone.", "Take fleabane with its flowers, pound very well with long stirring to the thickness of a plaster, and apply.", ""),
                    (15, "heading", "for the same", "For the same", ""),
                    (16, "body", "℞ Lotio cu[m] Lixiuio & ciner[ibus] petr[e] vel ligni fumi in quo ponatur par[um] Salis, Ebuli, & Sambuci. vide pa. 115. col. 2: ℞ Canopi.", "Take a lotion with lye and ashes of stone or wood-smoke in which a little salt, danewort, and elder are put. See p. 115, col. 2.", ""),
